From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] nfc: s3fwrn5: make the NCI_PROP_FW_CFG clock settings tunable

s3fwrn5_nci_rf_configure() sends NCI_PROP_FW_CFG with clk_type, clk_speed
and clk_req hardcoded to 0x01, 0xff and 0xff before it uploads the RF
registers. Those are not universal. The vendor stack on this phone sets
FW_CFG_CLK_SPEED to 0x11 in libnfc-sec-vendor.conf, extracted from the
LineageOS vendor image alongside the register blobs themselves.

The failure mode this produces is quiet and misleading: a part whose clock
is configured wrongly still answers NCI, still reports its protocols, still
accepts RF_DISCOVER with status 0, and simply never finds a card. Every
digital-side check passes. That is exactly the state this phone is in --
the registers upload cleanly, "rfreg configuration update: success", and an
EMV card and a MIFARE card passed repeatedly produce no interrupt at all.

Make the three settable so the value can be swept without rebuilding, since
the only way to find the right one here is to try. Defaults are unchanged,
so this is a no-op for every board that works today.

This is not yet known to fix anything. It is the one documented difference
left between what the vendor sends this part and what mainline sends it.

--- a/drivers/nfc/s3fwrn5/nci.c
+++ b/drivers/nfc/s3fwrn5/nci.c
@@ -45,6 +45,18 @@
 
 #define S3FWRN5_RFREG_SECTION_SIZE 252
 
+static int clk_type = 0x01;
+module_param(clk_type, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(clk_type, "NCI_PROP_FW_CFG clock type (default 0x01, external crystal)");
+
+static int clk_speed = 0xff;
+module_param(clk_speed, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(clk_speed, "NCI_PROP_FW_CFG clock speed (default 0xff; this phone's vendor stack uses 0x11)");
+
+static int clk_req = 0xff;
+module_param(clk_req, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(clk_req, "NCI_PROP_FW_CFG clock request (default 0xff)");
+
 int s3fwrn5_nci_rf_configure(struct s3fwrn5_info *info, const char *fw_name)
 {
 	struct device *dev = &info->ndev->nfc_dev->dev;
@@ -66,11 +78,19 @@
 	for (i = 0; i < fw->size; i += 4)
 		checksum += *((u32 *)(fw->data+i));
 
-	/* Set default clock configuration for external crystal */
-
-	fw_cfg.clk_type = 0x01;
-	fw_cfg.clk_speed = 0xff;
-	fw_cfg.clk_req = 0xff;
+	/*
+	 * Clock configuration.
+	 *
+	 * These three have always been hardcoded, and 0xff is not what every
+	 * board wants: the vendor stack for this phone sets FW_CFG_CLK_SPEED to
+	 * 0x11 in libnfc-sec-vendor.conf. A part whose clock is configured
+	 * wrongly will still answer NCI and still accept RF_DISCOVER -- the
+	 * digital side is fine -- and simply never see a card, which is exactly
+	 * the symptom that led here.
+	 */
+	fw_cfg.clk_type = clk_type;
+	fw_cfg.clk_speed = clk_speed;
+	fw_cfg.clk_req = clk_req;
 	ret = nci_prop_cmd(info->ndev, NCI_PROP_FW_CFG,
 		sizeof(fw_cfg), (__u8 *)&fw_cfg);
 	if (ret < 0)
